Problem:
A sheet of 8-inch by 10-inch paper is placed on top of a sheet of $8 \frac{1}{2}$-inch by 11-inch paper, as shown. What is the area of the region of overlap in square inches?

[asy]draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,8)--(0,8)--(0,0)--cycle,linewidth(2));
draw((0,8)--(8.5,8)--(8.5,11.5)--(0,11.5)--(0,8)--cycle,linewidth(2));

draw((8.5,0)--(8.5,8),dashed);
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  To find the area of the region of overlap, we need to determine the dimensions of the overlapping region. The overlapping region is a rectangle that is common to both sheets of paper.

The dimensions of the first sheet of paper are 8 inches by 10 inches.
The dimensions of the second sheet of paper are 8.5 inches by 11 inches.

The width of the overlapping region is the minimum of the widths of the two sheets, which is 8.5 inches.
The height of the overlapping region is the minimum of the heights of the two sheets, which is 8 inches.

The area of the overlapping region is given by the product of its width and height:
\[
\text{Area} = \text{width} \times \text{height} = 8.5 \text{ inches} \times 8 \text{ inches} = 68 \text{ square inches}
\]

Therefore, the area of the region of overlap is \boxed{68} square inches.
