A line passing through the point $(1,1,1)$ intersects the line defined by
\[\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}\]at $P,$ and intersects the line defined by
\[\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 3 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} + s \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}\]at $Q.$  Find point $Q.$

Solution:
answer:  The line passing through the point $(1,1,1)$ has the form
\[\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}.\]This line intersects the line defined by $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$ at $P = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.$

The line also intersects the line defined by $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 3 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} + s \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$ at $Q = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.$

Therefore, the point $Q$ is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}.$
