\[\tan \left( \frac{B - C}{2} \right) \tan \frac{A}{2} = \frac{1}{29} \quad \text{and} \quad \tan \left( \frac{C - A}{2} \right) \tan \frac{B}{2} = \frac{1}{27}.\]Find $\tan \left( \frac{A - B}{2} \right) \tan \frac{C}{2}.$

Solution:
answer:  We can use the half-angle formula for tangent, which states that

$$\tan \left( \frac{A - B}{2} \right) \tan \frac{C}{2} = \frac{\tan(A - B)}{\tan(C)}.$$

Given that $\tan \left( \frac{B - C}{2} \right) \tan \frac{A}{2} = \frac{1}{29}$ and $\tan \left( \frac{C - A}{2} \right) \tan \frac{B}{2} = \frac{1}{27}$, we can multiply these two equations to get

$$\tan \left( \frac{B - C}{2} \right) \tan \frac{A}{2} = \frac{1}{29} \cdot \frac{1}{27} = \frac{1}{803}.$$

Therefore, we have

$$\tan \left( \frac{A - B}{2} \right) \tan \frac{C}{2} = \boxed{\frac{1}{803}}.$$
